Z||000008||pherder||10-20-1999||08:54 PM||||Just wanted to remind everyone ...<BR>JC Whitney sells ST springs for the 84-87 ALOT cheaper than just about everyone else.<P>$79.99 a pair with 15% off (discount on catalog) if you buy $150 + of stuff (two pair ... $159.98 - 23.99=135.99 plus shipping.) Paul||||reg||4
Z||000009||lowCG||10-20-1999||09:13 PM||||I think the '88s may use a 5" coil in front,which is the same size as I put in my '87Fiero(from a '88 VW GTI,front)witha 1" spacer,Bilstien shocks.Have some other mods that alter the ride height,but in all,have a 1" lower than stock car now.<BR>Use '87 Mazda 626 fronts in the back with the upper spring seat removed,and the upper strut mount turned upside down,with Bilstiens.<BR>Rides firm,but not harsh.||||reg||1
Z||000010||mwbackus||10-21-1999||02:37 AM||||When did the Fiero Store stop selling Suspension Techniques lowering springs?  Everyone that I have questioned about the springs they purchased from them were quit positive that the box said "Suspension Techniques" right on it.  Is it possible that they are packaging cut springs in a bogus box?<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000011||88 Fiero||10-21-1999||11:00 AM||||MW...unless they know someone who does the coils for them... The weird thing about the Fiero Store is that they said they have the springs for the '88's...And that's hard to believe because ST's and Eibach's doesn't make any '88's...and a friend on mine bought a '88 springs from the Fiero Store and they still sell those springs...I'm confuse...Oh well...stick with the coils over...||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||10-21-1999||12:51 PM||||I'm in the process of install front Suspension Techniques sport lowering springs on my V8 Fiero. The quality and fit is good and the price is right, I think about $70 through JC Witless like Pherder said, BUT... it'll lower the front between 1-1/2 to 2 inches. If you want no more than one inch, you may have to try another option.||||reg||1
Z||000013||mwbackus||10-21-1999||01:02 PM||||I called up the Fiero Store this morning to inquire who makes the lowering springs they offer.  It seems on the 88's they do indeed come from a manufacturer other than the Suspension Techniques (They do have ST's for the pre-88 models).  This company is called KDB (or KBD??). Anyway, that explains the discrepancy between the springs, and why some have stated they received actual Suspension Techniques Springs from the Fiero Store.  I am not sure of all the options for coil overs on the 88 fronts, the only system I know of is from Ryane Motorsports and it requires changing to their extremely expensive tubular lower control arms.  But, Ryane does have a %15 off sale this month and that reduces the price of the 1 1/2" drop spindles to only $444. (I could almost rebuild my damn engine for that much $$).  BTW, they state that they may stop making stuff for the Fiero in the future, because all us Fiero owners do not buy up their overpriced stuff like hotcakes. :Þ<P>American Custom Engineering (ACE) <A HREF="http://www.americancustomeng.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.americancustomeng.com/</A> <BR>is advertising a monthly special: <BR>"88 ACE-Eibach Performance Spring Set  - $ 310.00" (note the "ACE-Eibach" terminology, I find that somewhat suspicious).<P>So, now that I have totally confused myself and others, I think I will give up on the idea of lowering the front of my Formula for awhile.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000014||Phantom Rage||10-21-1999||03:21 PM||||Hey thx all. I guess I'll give the fiero store a call.  The "Ace-Eibachs" seem a bit too expensive, even if they are true Eibachs. <BR>Thx again||||reg||12
